        "titel": "1. What governance is (and why everyone uses it)",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "**A word with many labels.** Governance has been called a buzzword, a fad, a framing device, a bridging concept, an umbrella concept, a slippery concept, an empty signifier, a weasel word, a fetish, a field, an approach, a theory and a perspective. \"It is almost as popular to lament the multiple, and sometimes ambiguous, meanings of governance, as it is to employ the term in creative ways.\"\n\n**Levi-Faur's definition.** In the Handbook, governance is **an interdisciplinary research agenda on order and disorder, efficiency and legitimacy**, in the context of the **hybridisation of modes of control** that produce fragmented and multidimensional order **within the state, by the state, without the state and beyond the state**.\n\n**Steering rather than power.** The literature follows Karl Deutsch's *The Nerves of Government* (1963): \"it might be profitable to look upon government somewhat less as a problem of power and somewhat more as a problem of **steering**.\" Institutional technocrats preach governance as a **technology of control**, not an instrument of power, and sometimes convince even suspicious governments to adopt it, at least on the surface.\n\n**A bridging concept.** Governance is one of the most important manifestations of **[[neo-institutionalism|Neo-institutionalism]]** (March and Olsen, 1984) and offers an integrative theme for the fragmented social sciences. Scholars from many disciplines share it because they recognise **the growing gap between the formal constitutional order and the way order is produced in everyday life**.\n\n**From marginal to everywhere.** The word was rarely used and \"nearly incomprehensible\" before the 1980s; it now appears in countless titles, journals and teaching programmes. It is still being translated: in Hebrew there was no agreed word, and Chinese scholars agreed by 2000 on **zhili** (\"rule\" plus \"management\"), which officials use in a more state-controlled sense than scholars.\n\n**The aim of the chapter**: to place governance in a broader understanding of **institutionalisation** and of the shift towards **poly-centred** polities, politics and policy, through **four ways of thinking about governance in relation to the state**, and to argue for one of them: [[Big Governance]]."
