        "titel": "2. Where the concept comes from",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "**Etymology.** From the Greek **[[kybernan|Kybernan]]**, to pilot, steer or direct, translated into Latin as **gubernare**: the root of both \"government\" and \"governance\". (In fourteenth-century France *gouvernance* meant royal officers; in Elizabethan England people spoke of the governance of the family.)\n\n**1950s-1970s: marginal.** The few papers on governance were mainly about **higher education** and **urban** governance: areas where hierarchical control does not capture much of the politics.\n\n**1979: the tipping point.** Oliver **Williamson**'s *Transaction-Cost Economics: The Governance of Contractual Relations* (1979) accounted for about 83 per cent of citations on governance in 1975-80. He studied how actors minimise **[[transaction costs|Transaction costs]]** and distinguished four types of governance: **market, unilateral, bilateral and trilateral**. His typology was not widely adopted, but his distinction between **[[markets and hierarchies|Markets and hierarchies]]** became entrenched (it was already used by Dahl and Lindblom in 1953).\n\n**The 1980s: corporate governance** dominated (eight of the ten most cited papers in 1981-85).\n\n**The 1990s: networks and a buzz-concept.** Against markets and hierarchies, the **network** became the third governance structure: Walter Powell's *Neither market nor hierarchy: network forms of organization* (1990) and Rod **Rhodes**'s *Policy networks: a British perspective* (1990). Political scientists had long studied governance beyond government without the name: **corporatism**, **private interest government**, **issue networks** and **policy styles**.\n\n**The numbers.** In the 1980s, 349 papers (3,609 citations); in the 1990s more than ten times as many (3,773 papers, 70,157 citations); in the 2000s, 18,648 papers. By 2006-09 they came from economics, management, political science, business, environmental studies, public administration and over fifty other fields. The influence of a few papers flattened out, which Levi-Faur reads as a healthy development."
